<p><span class="slidev-note-click-mark" data-clicks="1"></span> First, this has nothing to do with arrow functions versus function declarations. Both syntaxes can be used with either type of function.
<span class="slidev-note-click-mark" data-clicks="2"></span> Second, this issue applies equally to interfaces and types. The problem isn't with how we're defining our types, but with the method syntax itself.
<span class="slidev-note-click-mark" data-clicks="3"></span> Finally, remember that the issue is with the syntax we use to define the method, not with the type of function we're using.
<span class="slidev-note-click-mark" data-clicks="4"></span> In this example, you can see that we can mix and match arrow functions and function declarations with both syntaxes. The key is to use object property syntax, regardless of the function type.</p>
